{"name":"Mint Breath","hex":"#e3eee3","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A building or institution where money (originally, only coins) is produced under government licence.","A vast sum of money;  a large amount of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reproduce (coins), usually en masse, under licence.","To invent; to forge; to fabricate; to fashion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mint"},{"term":"breath","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act or process of breathing.","A single act of breathing in or out; a breathing of air."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["voiceless, surd; contrasting with voice (breath sounds, voice sounds)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breath"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mint (candy)","blocks":[{"text":"A mint or breath mint is a food item often consumed as an after-meal refreshment or before business and social engagements to improve breath odor. Mints are commonly believed to soothe the stomach, given their association with natural byproducts of the plant genus Mentha. Mints sometimes contain derivatives from plants such as peppermint oil or spearmint oil, or wintergreen from the plant genus Gaultheria. However, many of the most popular mints citing these natural sources contain none in their ingredient list or contain only trace amounts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Scotch_mints.JPG/330px-Scotch_mints.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mint_(candy)","wikidata":"Q5254978"},{"title":"Breathing","blocks":[{"text":"Breathing is the rhythmic process of inhaling air into the lungs providing oxygen, and exhaling the air removing carbon dioxide, in the gas exchange process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Real-time_MRI_-_Thorax.ogv/330px--Real-time_MRI_-_Thorax.